#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183997 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f421004-9c22-54e2-b3af-1470c50603c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, and Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/id #uuid "5da3337e-31d2-5f2d-b236-60088b4bf64d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3524" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = -\\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad\n\\cos v = \\dfrac {15} {17}, \\qquad\n\\sin v = \\dfrac {8} {17}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u + v\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u + v) = \\cos u \\cos v - \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{v}$, and $\\sin{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{15}{17})-(-\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{45}{85}+\\frac{32}{85} \\\\  & =-\\frac{13}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184079 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e97419-7fed-5bd0-9780-f34c6f05cfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/id #uuid "8877f6b8-8914-5022-9ec6-d4e8e0d3d159" :question/math-academy-id "e-9992" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad\n\\cos u = \\dfrac {3} {5}, \\qquad \n\\sin v = \\dfrac {9} {15}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u - v\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{u}$, and $\\sin{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{v,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 v = 1- \\sin^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{9}{15})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{9}^{2}}{{15}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{81}{225} \\\\  & =\\frac{225}{225}-\\frac{81}{225} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{225}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{225}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos v= \\dfrac{12}{15}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{12}{15})+(\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{9}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{75}+\\frac{36}{75} \\\\  & =\\frac{72}{75} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316339 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f421004-9c22-54e2-b3af-1470c50603c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, and Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8754972-d777-5921-8825-cab32ac9336e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316341} {:answer/id #uuid "b546072a-94eb-57ba-afce-a2056fc9363e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316342} {:answer/id #uuid "39a06dd3-c025-563f-aa1f-dc10e5c03f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316343} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a0d9cf-b5c1-57f3-874d-fe273eff8781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316344} {:answer/id #uuid "3cf21aca-aa4e-5424-900b-51f48eec337a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5a0d9cf-b5c1-57f3-874d-fe273eff8781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316344} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff4bbcc4-cd54-5e9f-b4cf-fa8854b44a5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1edeb94-5759-59d2-aa99-4326424b4123" :question/math-academy-id "q-141444" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{6}-b\\sqrt{3}}{15},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})(-\\frac{3}{5})+(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3})(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{15}+\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}-3\\sqrt{3}}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=4,b=3,$ and $a+b=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e97419-7fed-5bd0-9780-f34c6f05cfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0011dbd-59c1-5568-a956-986cb56d8086" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{325}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0011dbd-59c1-5568-a956-986cb56d8086" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36}{325}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316348} :answer-field/id #uuid "c450d36b-caa9-58f3-a179-7e8385c56270" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57bfb8e3-4136-5932-9bae-802bac135e24" :question/math-academy-id "q-271612" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{5}{13},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{24}{25}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡u=1-{cos}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{5}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{24}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{24}{25})-(\\frac{12}{13})(\\frac{7}{25}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{65}-\\frac{84}{325} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{325}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f421004-9c22-54e2-b3af-1470c50603c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, and Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cb49a13-1dbf-5a71-8be8-a12d785e7eb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cb49a13-1dbf-5a71-8be8-a12d785e7eb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316351} :answer-field/id #uuid "273dcf32-c19e-5e37-9409-dfe2ae9d72de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7b8d99f-5d99-55a7-b2bd-22265a5c88cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-271617" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, then $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{4}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{3}{5})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+(-\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{10}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e97419-7fed-5bd0-9780-f34c6f05cfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84d47e9b-443a-5391-bc89-c9c4406d363a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316354} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb29da0-92e0-52bb-ad87-086d9bcbfcae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84d47e9b-443a-5391-bc89-c9c4406d363a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316354} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad824e54-1fa7-550f-a047-a685d70438a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316353} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "662c13ba-4354-59a8-af8d-b346b23c1209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "662c13ba-4354-59a8-af8d-b346b23c1209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316357} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e02ac1-b064-5790-93b8-c98c63f28ae2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f7b0e36-eff7-557f-8199-5025d0189429" :question/math-academy-id "q-325846" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{2}\\,-\\sqrt{14}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive, we take\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{8}-\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{14}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e97419-7fed-5bd0-9780-f34c6f05cfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27378ddb-ffa9-59c3-a794-78e3ee507811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27378ddb-ffa9-59c3-a794-78e3ee507811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316360} :answer-field/id #uuid "eae73290-c920-57ec-b886-13f335f3541a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316359} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d6b6521-2ced-522e-b269-31fd704a5256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d6b6521-2ced-522e-b269-31fd704a5256" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316362} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a68254c-7510-52cd-b44b-d54509bb9fd4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2b1d186f-caff-5d01-9066-7cefce7050c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-325854" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{2}\\,+\\sqrt{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive, we take\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{10}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f421004-9c22-54e2-b3af-1470c50603c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, and Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a82828f-c3be-57b9-bb3b-cf249b00ffe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a82828f-c3be-57b9-bb3b-cf249b00ffe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316365} :answer-field/id #uuid "229246ff-c111-5a0d-a025-4be1d0a9b05d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316364} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85ca4ff0-ce72-5f24-9b5a-501d57109752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85ca4ff0-ce72-5f24-9b5a-501d57109752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316367} :answer-field/id #uuid "11df71b7-1a23-55b5-931f-fba31aeffd86" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b91fe6fa-a2c0-5d9e-9321-69d2787ceee6" :question/math-academy-id "q-325991" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=$ | $=$ | $-\\sqrt{5}\\,-\\,$ {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-2}} |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})(-\\frac{2}{3})-(-\\frac{1}{3})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{9}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{5}-4\\sqrt{2}}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f421004-9c22-54e2-b3af-1470c50603c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, and Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea320ccb-08ab-5997-827b-3b2de2d747b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea320ccb-08ab-5997-827b-3b2de2d747b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316370} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bf588cc-9eda-5ff0-bb11-6c9d491bacca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316369} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "359fac58-230d-596a-879b-621123f1b6d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "359fac58-230d-596a-879b-621123f1b6d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316372} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4ecf5c7-c7ef-5529-bf91-b1930fe9b697" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90b0fd28-fd46-5304-9b86-cd264d357979" :question/math-academy-id "q-325997" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{7}{25},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{24}{25},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{13}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} $\\sqrt{159}\\,-\\,$ {{field-2}} $\\sqrt{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $325$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{10}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{10}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{159}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{159}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{159}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe’re given that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{159}}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{7}{25})(\\frac{\\sqrt{159}}{13})+(\\frac{24}{25})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{13}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{159}}{325}-\\frac{24\\sqrt{10}}{325} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{159}-24\\sqrt{10}}{325}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e97419-7fed-5bd0-9780-f34c6f05cfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91b73259-15cc-551b-bb72-39d334dc51a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{84}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183582} {:answer/id #uuid "c762bf12-b1e6-5249-9aac-e301c9001f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{74}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183583} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3191d0-ff2b-5919-b490-9a88c6f906dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{84}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183584} {:answer/id #uuid "d3da2a44-a5fb-5096-84d9-892bdc2b8028" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{94}{97}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183585} {:answer/id #uuid "01304835-f6c5-5d9a-ae80-b8f633aa7c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{94}{97}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca3191d0-ff2b-5919-b490-9a88c6f906dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{84}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183584} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc9f13b7-9826-57a7-80fe-f4b56f9fa1f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f39d2c0-c3fb-5d82-bb15-c8596eec6f87" :question/math-academy-id "q-74457" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{15}{17}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{15}{17})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{15}^{2}}{{17}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{225}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{289}-\\frac{225}{289} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{289}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{289}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{8}{17}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{15}{17})+(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{8}{17}) \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{85}+\\frac{24}{85} \\\\  & =\\frac{84}{85}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f421004-9c22-54e2-b3af-1470c50603c8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the Second, Third, and Fourth Quadrants"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a2fcc6d-fe40-56d9-8153-cca28208264f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316375} {:answer/id #uuid "f1dff135-157c-5442-b831-9c5a4e6dcd1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7+\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316376} {:answer/id #uuid "31ee8a63-5e87-5453-b477-e876bdec4470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316377} {:answer/id #uuid "e0bde111-aa9f-5468-892e-52cbf07cbea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3+7\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316378} {:answer/id #uuid "0ba05f87-2add-588a-aa0a-99f38e040845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2+7\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31ee8a63-5e87-5453-b477-e876bdec4470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316377} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7388283-1db3-5a39-aedf-f7b08d2d85c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f19c0b6-fdce-5438-af4b-a47c18ae7e30" :question/math-academy-id "q-74458" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{12}{15},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{9}{15}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})(\\frac{12}{15})-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})(\\frac{9}{15}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12\\sqrt{2}}{30}-\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{30} \\\\  & =-\\frac{21\\sqrt{2}}{30} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7\\sqrt{2}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60e97419-7fed-5bd0-9780-f34c6f05cfd1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Cosine of a Composite Angle Using Angles in the First Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2413aec1-17d1-5204-a256-5cbea2b518e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316381} {:answer/id #uuid "18882871-1208-59c2-8d50-ccce922350d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316382} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0735b4-91b1-5126-80b4-be03e9f44353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316383} {:answer/id #uuid "574de433-9e44-53a2-ad08-9737fa620afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316384} {:answer/id #uuid "de5fdfa4-2cfd-5ca4-b9cf-6419d6c04030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "574de433-9e44-53a2-ad08-9737fa620afd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316384} :answer-field/id #uuid "155e9f71-563c-5ea5-8e71-4a317c03f6cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c420f71d-937d-5553-a82e-dad726247670" :question/math-academy-id "q-74459" :question/problem "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{3}{5},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{6}-b}{35},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,$ $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡u=1-{cos}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(2\\sqrt{6})^{2}}{{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{24}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{49}-\\frac{24}{49} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{49}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{5}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})(\\frac{4}{5})-(\\frac{5}{7})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{6}}{35}-\\frac{15}{35} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{6}-15}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=8,b=15,$ and $a+b=23.$"}]}
